(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-allergy-inflammation-quiz"]={"title":"Allergy, Inflammation & Dry Eye Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A toddler swallows a bottle of an imidazoline eye drop. Why does an agent that vasoconstricts locally cause central nervous system depression when ingested?","choices":["It acts on alpha-1 receptors both locally and systemically","It is metabolized to an opioid-like compound in the gut","Locally it acts on alpha-1 receptors, but systemically it targets alpha-2","It blocks rather than stimulates adrenergic receptors when absorbed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. That split is the whole reason the question is asked. Alpha-1 activation in the conjunctival vessels gives the local vasoconstriction; absorbed systemically the drug acts at alpha-2 receptors, and central alpha-2 agonism produces sedation, bradycardia and depressed respiration. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which conditions are given as the main uses of ocular glucocorticoids?","choices":["Bacterial conjunctivitis and corneal ulcer","Chronic dry eye with inflammation","Open angle glaucoma","Severe ocular allergy, anterior uveitis, external inflammatory disease and inflammation after surgery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All four are inflammation the nonsteroidals are not expected to control, which is what puts the steroid a step above them. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What do tear substitutes contain, and what is the purpose of the thickeners in them?","choices":["Only sterile water, the thickeners preventing evaporation","Antibiotic and thickener, to prevent secondary infection","Cyclosporine in a thickened base, to reduce inflammation","Electrolytes, surfactants and thickeners"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Same principle as a gel or ointment — the longer the preparation stays in contact, the more benefit it gives. Balanced salt solution, carboxymethylcellulose and polyvinyl alcohol are the over-the-counter examples. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A contact lens wearer has conjunctivitis. What is the advice about resuming lens wear?","choices":["Continue wearing them, since the drops will treat the eye anyway","Stop, and resume as soon as the drops are finished","Stop, and resume after 24 hours clear of inflammation","Stop permanently and switch to spectacles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. All three parts matter — stop, wait for a clear 24 hours, and deal with the lens itself. Eye makeup should go too, since it is the other reservoir. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which of these is an ocular antihistamine available over the counter?","choices":["Cromolyn","Ketotifen","Bromfenac","Loteprednol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is the one marked over the counter in the antihistamine list, which matters because it is what a patient may already have tried before they reach you. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which cells are the principal targets in ocular hypersensitivity, and what happens when immunoglobulin E binds them?","choices":["Neutrophils and macrophages; binding triggers phagocytosis","T lymphocytes; binding triggers interleukin-2 release","Mast cells and basophils, within seconds","Corneal epithelial cells; binding triggers prostaglandin release"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Immunoglobulin E binds Fc receptors, tyrosine kinases activate within 5 to 15 seconds, and histamine, platelet-activating factor and leukotrienes are released — producing vasodilation, swelling, redness and itch. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"How do ocular nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs reduce inflammation?","choices":["They inhibit phospholipase A2, preventing arachidonic acid formation","They stabilize mast cells against degranulation","They block cyclooxygenase","They inactivate the H1 receptor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They act one step below the steroids: the steroids stop arachidonic acid being liberated at all, while the nonsteroidals stop what is already liberated becoming prostaglandin. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What is meant by a soft steroid, and which agents are given as examples?","choices":["Fluorometholone, loteprednol and rimexolone","One given at a lower concentration — dexamethasone and prednisolone","One given by intravitreal injection — triamcinolone","One that does not cause cataract — difluprednate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Three of the listed agents are marked out as soft steroids for exactly that reason, which matters most in someone with a family history of pressure rise. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A patient has dry eye in the context of rheumatoid arthritis. What should be done first?","choices":["Treat the underlying disease","Start cyclosporine drops immediately","Insert punctal plugs","Prescribe a topical steroid pulse"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dry eyes are often a manifestation of something systemic — Sjogren syndrome, rheumatoid arthritis, vitamin A deficiency, Stevens-Johnson syndrome — and treating the eye without addressing the cause treats a symptom. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"How does cyclosporine improve chronic dry eye?","choices":["It thickens the tear film to prolong contact time","It inhibits interleukin-2 production","It blocks cyclooxygenase in the lacrimal gland","It occludes the lacrimal drainage system pharmacologically"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Less inflammation in the lacrimal gland means increased tear production, with improved vision and comfort. Its indication is chronic dry eye associated with inflammation — keratoconjunctivitis sicca. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What are the main uses of ocular glucocorticoids?","choices":["First-line treatment of all allergic conjunctivitis","Bacterial keratitis, to limit scarring","Chronic dry eye as maintenance therapy","Severe allergy, uveitis and post-surgical inflammation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. They are generally reserved for refractory symptoms, and can be given topically or intraocularly depending on the indication. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Ocular H1 agents are described as not really antagonists. What are they?","choices":["Non-competitive antagonists that bind irreversibly","Partial agonists producing a submaximal histamine response","Mast cell stabilizers that prevent histamine release","Inverse agonists, still competing with histamine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A true antagonist would simply occupy the site; an inverse agonist drives the receptor into an inactive state. They remain competitive with histamine, so the effect still depends on relative concentrations. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"For which patients are ointments preferred over drops, and what must they be warned about?","choices":["Contact lens wearers, warned to keep lenses in to hold the ointment in place","Children and poor compliers; vision blurs for twenty minutes","Adults with glaucoma, warned that ointment raises pressure","Patients with dry eye, warned that ointment worsens tear film stability"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ointment works even if it only reaches the lashes, because it still makes contact with the eye — which is exactly why it suits a squirming child. The trade-off is blurring, so no driving until it clears. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which adverse effect of the ocular nonsteroidals matters most in a patient already being watched for glaucoma?","choices":["Reduced tear production","A rise in intraocular pressure","Changes in eyelash length","Bradycardia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The drug given for inflammation pushes the very pressure the glaucoma treatment exists to lower, so the two work against each other. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which adverse effect of ocular antihistamines is worth mentioning to a patient who already has dry eyes?","choices":["Increased ocular dryness","Raised intraocular pressure","Permanent iris color change","Corneal ulceration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Along with irritation and headache, antihistamines can worsen dryness — which matters when dry eye is the background problem, since the treatment can aggravate the complaint. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What should a patient starting cyclosporine drops be warned about?","choices":["Ocular burning, which affects about one in six","Permanent darkening of the iris","An increased risk of cataract","Rebound redness if the drops are stopped suddenly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Burning occurs in roughly seventeen percent, along with foreign body sensation and blurred vision. Warning about it up front is what keeps a patient on a drug that takes time to work. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"For which patient are mast cell stabilizers described as most useful?","choices":["Predictable seasonal allergy, intolerant of alternatives","Someone needing relief from an acute allergic flare","Someone with postoperative ocular inflammation","Someone with bacterial conjunctivitis and marked itching"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Their weaknesses — 5 to 14 days to full effect and often four times daily dosing — are tolerable if the season is predictable and can be pre-empted, and if the alternatives are not an option. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 47"}]};
